WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.355
Hi, I'm Cider!

00:00:02.355 --> 00:00:05.195
Kimi K3 is the largest open model yet.

00:00:05.195 --> 00:00:08.035
What's interesting is how its parameters are spent.

00:00:09.517 --> 00:00:13.878
K3 reworks how information flows three ways: along the sequence, up the

00:00:13.878 --> 00:00:16.059
layers, and out to its experts.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:17.876
Each makes compute go further.

00:00:19.292 --> 00:00:24.092
A token climbs ninety-three layers: attention, then a mixture of experts, each time.

00:00:24.512 --> 00:00:27.556
Three in four attention layers are Kimi Delta Attention.

00:00:27.556 --> 00:00:31.614
It keeps a fixed-size memory: each token erases a little and writes

00:00:31.614 --> 00:00:33.305
its own key and value.

00:00:33.725 --> 00:00:37.705
Every fourth is Gated MLA, full attention for exact recall.

00:00:37.705 --> 00:00:42.481
None use positional encoding; the delta layers carry order through their decay.

00:00:42.901 --> 00:00:46.625
Then the experts: sixteen of eight hundred and ninety-six, plus two shared,

00:00:46.625 --> 00:00:49.418
working in a latent space half the model's width.

00:00:49.838 --> 00:00:54.143
Attention Residuals let each layer pick from earlier blocks and the embedding,

00:00:54.143 --> 00:00:57.014
instead of piling everything into one running sum.

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:01.515
The router picks one more expert than it needs.

00:01:01.515 --> 00:01:04.968
That extra score is the cutoff a rival expert had to beat.

00:01:05.388 --> 00:01:09.502
Each expert's margins, its score minus the cutoff, are binned locally, and

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:12.244
one all-reduce sums the bins across the cluster.

00:01:12.664 --> 00:01:16.631
Its next bias is read straight off a quantile, which hands every

00:01:16.631 --> 00:01:17.953
expert its target load.

00:01:17.953 --> 00:01:19.606
No auxiliary loss, no knob.

00:01:20.883 --> 00:01:24.962
Together, K3 matches K2's validation loss with about two and a

00:01:24.962 --> 00:01:26.446
half times fewer FLOPs.

00:01:28.142 --> 00:01:30.451
Parameter count is a spec-sheet figure.

00:01:30.451 --> 00:01:33.531
More capability per FLOP is the engineering result.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.731
To recap: a memory that never grows, residuals that choose, and experts

00:01:39.731 --> 00:01:41.175
balanced by a quantile.

00:01:41.595 --> 00:01:43.704
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:43.704 --> 00:01:44.307
I'm Cider.

00:01:44.307 --> 00:01:44.608
Bye!
